{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Ring.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Ring.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"polynomial ring polynomial ring","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Ring.md","fileName":"Polynomial Ring.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Polynomial Ring","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 19:40\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Ring and Ideal Foundations]] · [[Polynomial Rings and Factorization]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Ring and Ideal Foundations","Polynomial Rings and Factorization","Field","Commutative Ring","Integral Domain","Gröbner Basis","commutativealgebra.pdf","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Ring.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Ring.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"polynomial ring polynomial ring","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Ring.md","fileName":"Polynomial Ring.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Polynomial Ring","heading":"Polynomial Ring","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A polynomial ring K[x₁,…,xₙ] consists of finite sums of monomials whose coefficients lie in a base ring or [[Field]]. Addition combines coefficients and multiplication uses the exponent laws, making the collection a [[Commutative Ring]].\n\nWhen the coefficients form a field, the polynomial ring is an [[Integral Domain]]. Its ideals encode systems of polynomial equations and can be studied computationally through a [[Gröbner Basis]].\n\nFor a one-variable polynomial ring A[x], the source defines coefficientwise addition and convolution-style multiplication, then proves the ring properties inherited from A. When A is an integral domain, degrees of nonzero products add and A[x] is also a domain.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Ring and Ideal Found